<div class="cw-rules-list">
    <p class="cw-rules-intro">
        The Game of Life is not your typical computer game. It is a cellular automaton,
        and was invented by Cambridge mathematician John Conway.
    </p>
    <p class="cw-rules-intro">
        This game became widely known when it was mentioned in an article published by
        Scientific American in 1970. It consists of a grid of cells which, based on a few
        mathematical rules, can live, die or multiply. Depending on the initial conditions,
        the cells form various patterns throughout the course of the game.
    </p>

    <h3 class="cw-rules-section-head" style="margin-top:14px">Simulation Mechanics</h3>
    @foreach (var (label, desc) in SettingsRulesData.SimulationEvents)
    {
        <div class="cw-rules-entry cw-rules-event">
            <div class="cw-rules-text">
                <span class="cw-rules-name">@label</span>
                <span class="cw-rules-desc">@desc</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    <h3 class="cw-rules-section-head" style="margin-top:14px">Cell Types</h3>
    @foreach (var (name, desc) in SettingsRulesData.CellRules)
    {
        <div class="cw-rules-entry">
            <div class="cw-sprite-wrap">
                <img class="cw-cell-sprite" src="Assets/Sprites/Cell_@(name).jpg?v=3" alt="@name" />
                <img class="cw-sprite-zoom-popup" src="Assets/Sprites/Cell_@(name).jpg?v=3" alt="" />
            </div>
            <div class="cw-rules-text">
                <span class="cw-rules-name">@name</span>
                <span class="cw-rules-desc">@desc</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</div>
